{"qid":"Q9903","names":["Ming Dynasty"],"title":"Ming Dynasty","endonym":{"native":"大明","lang":"zh"},"capsule":"The Ming dynasty was founded in 1368 when Zhu Yuanzhang (the Hongwu Emperor), a peasant who rose through rebellion, drove the Mongols north and established his capital at Nanjing, later moved to Beijing by his son the Yongle Emperor in 1421. The Yongle Emperor (r. 1402–1424) also sponsored the seven great voyages of Zheng He (1405–1433), projecting Chinese prestige across Southeast Asia, the Indian Ocean, and East Africa. The dynasty's middle period saw construction of the current Great Wall and the development of a prosperous commercial economy, but the late 16th century brought fiscal crisis, Japanese piracy (wokou), and costly wars against Japanese invasions of Korea (1592–1598). The dynasty collapsed under combined pressure from Li Zicheng's peasant rebellion, which took Beijing in April 1644, and Manchu forces who then seized the capital and established the Qing dynasty.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Administrative provincial boundaries are well documented by Ming geographical gazetteers (difangzhi) and the Da Ming Huidian; the Great Wall frontier is archaeologically and documentarily attested; tributary and nominal frontier zones in Mongolia and Central Asia are less certain; high confidence for the Chinese heartland.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Ming dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Ming-dynasty-Chinese-history"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Ming dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ming_dynasty"}],"britannica":"topic/Ming-dynasty-Chinese-history",